  "failure_investigation_50c": {
    "held_out_run": "T700-12k-50C-T1",
    "findings": [
      "Plausible statistical contributor at low-load ramp step 0: In step 0 (normal load ~0.077 N), observed COF was 1.1636 in T1 versus 1.63 to 2.02 in training 50C sweeps. The model predicted 1.9496, producing a residual of +0.7861 and squared error of 0.6179. This single observation accounts for 65.0% of the run's total sum of squared errors (0.9504), establishing a theoretical lower bound on the run's RMSE of sqrt(0.6179 / 14) = 0.2101, which is fully consistent with the actual run RMSE of 0.2605.",
      "Steps 3-9 and overall functional tracking: Across ramp steps 3 through 9 (loads 3.5 N to 39.2 N), model predictions track observations within an MAE of 0.0300 (errors between 0.011 and 0.051) with a correlation of r = 0.9867. Across all 14 steps of the run, the overall Pearson correlation is r = 0.9520, confirming that the model captures the functional shape of the load-friction curve.",
      "Plausible explanation vs proven root cause: While Step 0 statistically dominates the squared error, one unusual observation alone does not prove that experimental measurement noise caused the poor score. Physical factors (e.g. sizing tack, static friction transitions) cannot be ruled out without independent specimen replication.",
      "Holdout sample size vulnerability: The holdout partition contains only a single 50C run (14 rows), making the aggregate 50C holdout score (-0.7446) entirely dependent on that single sweep.",
      "Sensitivity under leave-one-run-out: Across all 5 runs of 50C under LOGO, 3 runs achieve R^2 >= 0.70 (T2: 0.91, T4: 0.76, T5: 0.71), while T1 (-1.04) and T3 (-0.39) drop negative, yielding a mean R^2 of 0.1901. In contrast, 60E achieves R^2 > 0.81 across every single run (mean R^2 = 0.8572)."
    ],
    "implication": "Predictions for T700-12k-50C carry substantially higher uncertainty and residual risk than T700-12k-60E. Recommendations or predictions for 50C must be treated with particular caution."
  },
